carla_docs: Hi all — docs PR #4213 is blocked on a runtime behavior clarification. The SDK example for streaming + function-calling currently shows a simple retry wrapper, but during an intermittent 5xx the example ends up replaying token output and producing duplicated tokens when the client reopens the stream. CI preview is green so I need a runtime/SDK confirmation before we merge. ping @ben_runtime @emily_sdk

ben_runtime: Thanks for the ping. Can you paste the snippet? Streaming retry semantics can be subtle (we have two codepaths: streaming via SSE/websocket and non-streaming HTTP).

carla_docs: Sure — here's the minimal snippet we included in the PR:

```
// JS SDK streaming example (simplified)
const stream = await client.responses.stream({ model: 'redwood-std', input: prompt, functions: [summarizeFn] });
try {
  for await (const chunk of stream) {
    if (chunk.type === 'function_call') handleFunctionCall(chunk);
    else processText(chunk.text);
  }
} catch (e) {
  // naive retry
  await wait(200);
  return client.responses.stream({ model: 'redwood-std', input: prompt, functions: [summarizeFn] });
}
```

carla_docs: The problem we saw in a real repro is duplicated text after the retry — the runtime returns overlapping tokens because the client re-requested from the start. We wanted to document the recommended approach for resumable streaming or safe retries.

emily_sdk: We intentionally left out resumable streaming docs because SDK behavior differs by transport. Our Node SDK does not automatically dedupe on retry for streaming requests. For non-streaming requests we have built-in idempotency via `request_id` headers, but streaming currently expects the app to handle function-call state and deduping.

ben_runtime: Exactly. Runtime does not provide a server-side resume token for arbitrary streamed deltas today. For function-calling we keep function-call state in the server for the duration of the request, but once the connection is closed we don't offer a resume endpoint. So naive retry will re-run generation from the beginning and may re-emit already-sent tokens.

dan_reviewer: That means the docs PR needs to either change the example to show a client-side deterministic retry (idempotent request with `request_id` + checkpointing) or downgrade the snippet to non-retrying streaming and add a note that streaming should not be retried automatically. Also call out that function-calling side effects must be guarded.

flo_qa: For the migration guide: we should add a short section recommending one of two patterns — (1) use non-streaming responses with `request_id` for server-side idempotency if you need automatic retries, or (2) for streaming, persist the last-acknowledged token index on the client and use a resumed prompt / delta offset to continue (with an example of a small resume protocol).

carla_docs: OK — that gives us 3 changes: update the snippet to remove naive retry, add a small non-streaming example using `request_id` header, and add a short note in the streaming section that runtime has no resume token and SDK won't dedupe streaming retries. I'll draft.

emily_sdk: I can post an SDK patch tomorrow to add a small helper `streamWithCheckpoint` in examples repo and link to it from docs. Also will add text to clarify transport differences (SSE vs HTTP chunking).

ben_runtime: I'll add a short blurb to the runtime changelog about this limitation and flag the product page so customers searching for "resume streaming" find the note. If you want me to answer any more technical Qs for the docs wording, ping me.

devex-bot: deploy-preview created: https://deploy-preview.redwood-inference/docs/4213 — preview will update in ~2m.

carla_docs: Great — thanks everyone. I'll update the PR now to remove the naive retry and add the two recommended patterns. Once emily posts the helper link I'll add it to the snippet and re-run the preview. Assigning @dan_reviewer for final sign-off.

dan_reviewer: SGTM. Please @mention me when ready.

carla_docs: Will do — marking this thread resolved for now. Thanks :thumbsup:
